  "worked_solution": "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n2\\,\u2223\\,{5}^{n}+{9}^{n}+2\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into the expression.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{5}^{1}+{9}^{1}+2=16,\\,\\text{and}\\,2\\,\u2223\\,16\n$$\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have that\n\n$$\n2\\,\u2223\\,{5}^{k}+{9}^{k}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)\u21d2P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n2\\,\u2223\\,{5}^{k+1}+{9}^{k+1}+2.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the expression using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is a multiple of $2.$\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)\u21d2P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $2\\,\u2223\\,{5}^{n}+{9}^{n}+2$ for all $n\\in N.$",
